/-!
# Geometrically normal algebras over a field

Let `k` be a field and let `A` be a commutative `k`-algebra. Then `A` is *geometrically normal*
over `k` when the base change `k' ⊗[k] A` is a normal ring — every localization at a prime ideal
is an integrally closed integral domain, Definition 00GV — for every field extension `k'/k`. The
condition is a condition on all extensions of `k`, transcendental ones included, but by Lemma 037Z
it is already detected by much less: by the finitely generated extensions, by the finite purely
inseparable extensions, and indeed by the single ring `k^{perf} ⊗[k] A` for a perfect closure
`k^{perf}` of `k`. Taking `k' = k` shows that a geometrically normal algebra is a normal ring, and
the converse fails: over `k = 𝔽_p(t)` the field `k[x]/(x^p - t)` is a normal ring which is not
even geometrically reduced. Over a perfect field the two notions agree.

## Main definitions

* `Algebra.IsGeometricallyNormal`: the predicate on a commutative algebra over a field saying that
  every base change of it to a field extension of the base is a normal ring.

## Implementation notes

The source defines geometric normality by declaring that "the equivalent conditions of Lemma 037Z
hold", so the definition is a choice of one of the four items of that lemma, and the remaining
three are criteria for it, obtained from `Algebra.isNormalRing_tensorProduct_tfae`. The item chosen
is the first, normality of `k' ⊗[k] A` for every field extension `k'/k`, because that one is the
meaning of the adverb "geometrically" and needs no auxiliary data: items (2) and (3) restrict the
quantifier, and item (4) speaks about a perfect closure, whose statement needs an exponential
characteristic `p` and a model of `k^{perf}` as extra arguments. Normality of
`AlgebraicClosure k ⊗[k] A` — the shape Mathlib's sibling `Algebra.IsGeometricallyReduced` takes
for reducedness — is an equivalent condition as well, but it is not one of the source's four: it
implies item (4) only through descent of normality along the faithfully flat ring map
`k^{perf} ⊗[k] A → AlgebraicClosure k ⊗[k] A` (Lemma 033G), so taking it as the definition would
put a proof between the definition and the source's list.

The test field ranges over `Type u`, the universe of `k`, exactly as in item (1) of the statement
formalized for Lemma 037Z, and this is the source's condition for extensions in every universe: an
element of `k'' ⊗[k] A` for `k''/k` in an arbitrary universe already lies in the image of
`k₀ ⊗[k] A` for the finitely generated subextension `k₀/k` generated by the finitely many elements
of `k''` occurring in a representative, `k₀` is `k`-isomorphic to a field in `Type u`, and
`k₀ ⊗[k] A → k'' ⊗[k] A` is injective because `A` is flat over the field `k`. Quantifying over a
free universe variable instead would be *weaker*, and would make the predicate vacuous for a field
`k` in `Type 1` of cardinality above the continuum, no field in `Type 0` carrying a `k`-algebra
structure at all.

The predicate is a `class` with a single field, as Mathlib's sibling
`Algebra.IsGeometricallyReduced` is and as the definition of a geometrically connected algebra
(Definition 037T) is, so that geometric normality of `A` is available to instance search at each of
the base changes it governs, and so that its projection
`Algebra.IsGeometricallyNormal.isNormalRing_tensorProduct`, which produces the normality of a named
base change, and its constructor, which asks for exactly that, are what a reader uses in place of
unfolding it. `IsNormalRing` is a plain predicate rather than a class, so the projection is not
registered as an instance.

`A` is assumed commutative, as in the source, since normality is a condition on the localizations
of a commutative ring, and base changes are written with the extension on the left, `k' ⊗[k] A`, as
in Mathlib, where the source writes `k' ⊗_k A`. The base is a field, as in the source; the
generalization to a base commutative ring, phrased at the residue fields of the primes as Mathlib's
sibling phrases geometric reducedness, is a different notion and is not this definition.

Faithfulness, on the four points a reader should check against the source.
(1) No hypothesis is dead: `Field k`, `CommRing A` and `Algebra k A` are precisely what is needed
to form the base changes `k' ⊗[k] A` and their localizations at primes, and the class has a single
field, which is the source's condition; nothing restricts `A` beyond commutativity, and no
finiteness or Noetherian hypothesis is smuggled in.
(2) The scalars and the base are the source's: every tensor product is over `k`, each test field is
an extension of `k` through its own `Algebra k` instance, and normality is asked of `k' ⊗[k] A` as
a ring — integral closedness of each of *its* prime localizations inside *its* fraction field — not
of `A` inside `Q(A)`.
(3) The source names no canonical map in this definition, and the statement pins the structure it
does name: the extension `k'/k` is the one its `Algebra k` instance gives, so the base change is a
determined ring rather than an unspecified one, and the condition is asserted for every such `k'`,
not merely for some normal base change.
(4) The instantiation is not degenerate: `A = k[X]` is geometrically normal over any field `k`,
since `k'[X]` is a normal domain for every field `k'`, while `A = k[x]/(x^p - t)` over
`k = 𝔽_p(t)` is not, its base change to `k^{perf}` having the nonzero nilpotent
`t^{1/p} ⊗ 1 - 1 ⊗ x`; and `k' = k` gives `IsNormalRing A`, so the predicate is neither vacuous nor
implied by normality of `A`.

## References

* [Stacks 0380](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0380), Definition 10.166.2, in the section
  "Geometrically normal algebras"; a normal ring is Definition 00GV, and the four equivalent
  conditions the definition refers to are Lemma 037Z.
-/

@[expose] public section

open scoped TensorProduct



namespace Algebra

/-- A commutative algebra `A` over a field `k` is *geometrically normal* over `k` if the base
change `k' ⊗[k] A` is a normal ring for every field extension `k'/k`. By Lemma 037Z it is enough
to ask this for the finitely generated extensions, or for the finite purely inseparable ones, or
for a single perfect closure of `k`. -/
@[stacks 0380 "The first of the four equivalent conditions of Lemma 037Z, a normal ring being
  spelled by the library's `IsNormalRing` (Definition 00GV); the other three are criteria for it,
  through `Algebra.isNormalRing_tensorProduct_tfae`.", ega "IV 6.7.6"]
public class IsGeometricallyNormal (k : Type u) (A : Type v) [Field k] [CommRing A]
    [Algebra k A] : Prop where
  /-- The base change of `A` to every field extension of `k` is a normal ring. -/
  isNormalRing_tensorProduct (k' : Type u) [Field k'] [Algebra k k'] : IsNormalRing (k' ⊗[k] A)

end Algebra

end
